    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1058">

                <pb facs="1211" n="1099" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg1099"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">895.63 Or 4/48</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1340</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-10">June 10, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received June
                        29.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to this Embassy’s
                    despatch No. 992, dated October 2, 1934,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1058-f1"/> in
                    regard to the demand of the taxation officials of the Chosen Government General
                    to examine the books of the Oriental Consolidated Mining Company, for the
                    purpose of assessing income tax upon the employees of the Company, and to the
                    Department’s instruction No. 645, dated December 3, 1934,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d1058-f2"/> authorizing the American Consul at Seoul to address a
                    letter to the Chosen Government General on the subject.</p>
                <p>According to a letter from the American Consul at Seoul dated May 17, 1935, (a
                    copy of which was sent to the Department<anchor n="3" xml:id="d1058-f3"/>) the
                    Chosen Government General refuses to relinquish its claim to the right to
                    examine the books for income taxation purposes, and the Consul suggests that, in
                    order to avoid unpleasantness, the Company might keep a separate ledger of
                    disbursements for salaries and exhibit this book voluntarily to the taxation
                    authorities. If the Department perceives no objection to this procedure, the
                    Embassy will instruct the Consul to suggest the plan to the Company.<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d1058-f4"/></p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph C. Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1058-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1058-f1-content">
